# Python in Excel SPC functions

SPC (statistical process control) functions are control charts and process-limit tools (`xmr`, `xbar_r`, `xbar_s`, `ewma`, `cusum`, `capability_report`, `process_shift_detection`, and later Shewhart / CUSUM / EWMA / capability tools). They are not time-series forecasting and not sampling.

## Init files

Copy the `def` and quoted call into **only**:

1. @source/python-in-excel/init/SPC.py (SPC-only Initialization)

Do **not** also add SPC functions to @source/python-in-excel/init/PaulPythonLibrary.py. Paul is the **general** init for functions that do not belong in a specialized collection. Do not add SPC functions to @source/python-in-excel/init/TimeSeries.py, @source/python-in-excel/init/Sampling.py, or @source/python-in-excel/init/DefaultInitialization.py.

`SPC.py` is a complete Initialization: Excel default imports, then SPC `def`s only.

Add a `contents()` row in `SPC.py` in the same change. See @.cursor/rules/python-in-excel-init-contents.mdc.

```python
# ❌ BAD — SPC function also copied into PaulPythonLibrary.py or TimeSeries.py
# ❌ BAD — def added, contents() not updated
# ✅ GOOD — same def in functions/name.py and SPC.py only; contents() row with call signature
```

Then follow @.cursor/rules/python-in-excel.mdc (function file, PythonMap, README, CHANGELOG, tests, docs).
